  journal  = "J. Clin. Epidemiol.",
  volume   =  129,
  pages    = "167--171",
  abstract = "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE: There is considerable actual and
              potential waste in research. Using evidence-based research (EBR)
              can ensure the value of a new study. The aim of this article, the
              third in a series, is to describe an EBR approach to putting
              research results into context. STUDY DESIGN AND SETTING: EBR is
              the use of prior research in a systematic and transparent way to
              inform a new study so that it is answering questions that matter
              in a valid, efficient, and accessible manner. In this third and
              final article of a series, we describe how to use the context of
              existing evidence to reach and present a trustworthy and useful
              conclusion when reporting results from a new clinical study.
              RESULTS: We describe a method, the EBR approach, that by using a
              systematic and transparent consideration of earlier similar
              studies when interpreting and presenting results from a new
