              distributions. We show how the ROC curve is an alternative way to
              present risk distributions of diseased and non-diseased
              individuals and how the shape of the ROC curve informs about the
              overlap of the risk distributions. For example, ROC curves are
              rounded when the prediction model included variables with similar
              effect on disease risk and have an angle when, for example, one
              binary risk factor has a stronger effect; and ROC curves are
