@ARTICLE{Streiner2018statistics,
  title     = "Statistics commentary series: Commentary no. 26: Dealing with
               outliers",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J. Clin. Psychopharmacol.",
  publisher = "journals.lww.com",
  volume    =  38,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "170--171",
  abstract  = "P arametric statistical tests, the ones we use most often, assume
               that there is some underlying distribution of the data, usually
               the normal, or Gaussian, one. (The term ‘parametric’ comes from
               the fact that this distribution is defined by two parameters: the
               mean and the standard deviation (SD).) By definition, 99.7\% of
               the values will fall between −3 and +3 SDs. But sometimes we
               encounter extreme values that fall outside of these limits; data
               points that are called ‘outliers.’ This paper will address a
               number of points relating to outliers: (1) why outliers arise;
               (2) why they are a problem; (3) how to detect them; (4) whether
               we should keep or discard them; and (5) how to handle them if we
               feel that the outliers are valid measures. It should be noted
               that this definition of an outlier – a data point that lies
               outside ±3 SDs – is perhaps the most widely used one but is by no
               means the only one. Aguinis et al. list 14 different ways in
               which authors have defined an outlier. Outliers may appear in a
               data set for any of a number of reasons. I recently analyzed the
               data for one study which involved recording the number of steps
               taken each day by children with a medical condition. One value
               was entered as 200306 instead of 20306; a simple, and all too
               common error of holding down the 0 key a fraction of a second too
               long. Other data entry errors may involve the transposition of
               numbers; entering an age as 91 instead of 19. Another source of
               problems comes from missing data. Good practice dictates that if
               a data value is missing, this should be coded in the data set
               rather than leaving the cell empty. Different codes could be used
               to reflect the reason for missingness, such as 999 indicating
               that the question was omitted; 998 that the person refused to
               answer; and 997 that the question did not apply. A problem
               arises, though, if you forget to tell the program that these
               values reflect missing data, in which case the computer will
               churn away merrily, interpreting these as valid responses. But
               not all extreme values are erroneous.We know, for example, that a
               disorder such as Alzheimer’s disease may consist of multiple
               subgroups, each reflecting a different etiology. If a sample of
               patients contained a small number of people with the rare
               mutation of the gene coding for the β-amyloid precursor protein
               which results in early disease onset, then the variable ‘age at
               diagnosis’ may seem to have 1 or 2 anomalously low values. These
               would not be errors, but would reflect the heterogeneity of the
               disorder. To see why outliers may be a problem, consider the
               following 10 numbers, which have been put in rank order: 2 5 13
               22 24 29 32 33 35 360 If we assume that the last value should
               really have been 36 and the 0 at the end was an input error, the
               actual values of the mean and SD would be 23.1 and 12.5. However,
               with the outlier left in, the mean is 55.5, which is larger than
               9 of the 10 values, and is obviously not a good estimate of where
               most of the data lie. Similarly, the SD, at 107.6, is a gross
               over-estimate of the spread of most of the numbers. Depending on
               where the outliers sit in relation to the other values, they can
               either strengthen or weaken the influence of that variable in a
               multiple regression, and similarly make the overall regression
               equation more or less significant, and the same holds true for
               all other statistical procedures. There are a number of ways to
               determine if there are any outliers, legitimate or illegitimate,
               in a data set, both visually and statistically. The easiest is to
               simply make a box plot. As we discussed in an earlier article,
               this will reveal the existence of extreme values, and many
               programs will actually give the case number of the offending data
               points. However, bear in mind that there is a problem if you
               blindly accept the computer’s output. It identifies an outlier as
               any value that is greater than 1.5 times the inter-quartile range
               (IQR), based on Tukey’s book, Exploratory Data Analysis. Tukey
               later changed his mind because this criterion labeled too many
               data points as extreme, and later work showed that the cut-off
               should actually be 2.2 times the IQR. Unfortunately, most
               programs still use the original value and there is noway to
               change it, so you’ll have to do the calculation by hand. Another
               technique that can be used would be to have the computer print
               out the highest and lowest value for each variable (again,
               something that should be done routinely before analyzing the data
               in any study) and seeing if anything looks amiss.",
  month     =  jun,
  year      =  2018,
  url       = "http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/JCP.0000000000000865",
  doi       = "10.1097/JCP.0000000000000865",
  pmid      =  29578889,
  issn      = "0271-0749,1533-712X",
  language  = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Streiner2018statistics-0002,
  title     = "Statistics commentary series: Commentary no. 27: {P}-hacking",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J. Clin. Psychopharmacol.",
  publisher = "journals.lww.com",
  volume    =  38,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "286--288",
  abstract  = "R esearchers live and die by the famous (or infamous) cut-off of
               P ≤ 0.05 – if the results of the study show a P level less than
               or equal to 0.05, then we can submit the paper and fame,
               promotion, and tenure will be ours; larger than 0.05, and will we
               have nothing to show for our time and effort except heartbreak,
               despondency, and a file drawer full of useless data. Although
               many have often pointed out the arbitrary nature of this
               criterion and have called for its demise, it, like the phoenix,
               keeps rising from the ashes, only to live again. (Others have
               likened it tomosquitos – annoying and impossible to swat away.)
               There aremany legitimate thingswe can do tomaximize our chances
               of getting significant results: design and execute the study
               well, use reliable and valid outcome measures, calculate the
               sample size beforehand to insure adequate power, use the
               appropriate statistics, and so forth. And, with so much resting
               on crossing that magical threshold, it is almost fatuous to say
               that people have come up with not so legitimate methods, of which
               “P-hacking” (a term introduced by Simmons, Nelson, and Simonsohn)
               is perhaps the most wide-spread (we’ll leave aside faking the
               data and plagiarism, as they are totally beyond the pale, but do
               in fact represent the reasons the majority of articles are
               retracted). P-hacking – the relentless search for statistical
               significance – comes in many guises. The most pervasive form is
               also referred to as “data dredging,” “significance chasing,”
               “data snooping,” or “going on a fishing expedition,” and refers
               to combing throughmasses of data, looking for significance, most
               often without any hypotheses guiding the search. Although data
               dredging has been with us ever since people measured more than
               twovariables at a time, it got a boost (if that’s the correct
               term) with the convergence of desk-top computers, “big data”
               amassed by corporations, and the ability to collect thousands of
               variables simultaneously, as with the human genome or
               computerized scans of the brain. The reason it is highly
               suspicious goes back to a point discussed in a previous article.
               If the null hypothesis is true (ie, nothing is going on), and we
               run k tests with an alpha level of 0.05, then the probability of
               finding at least one significant finding (P1) is given by the
               formula: P1 = 1 – 0.95 . Run 10 tests and P1 is 40\%; with 20 it
               is 64\%; and with 100 tests, it is greater than 99\%; that is, if
               you do enough tests, you are virtually guaranteed that something
               will emerge as significant. This was delightfully shown in an
               article by Puget, who conclusively showed that green dice are
               loaded. He (or, more accurately, his computer) rolled 10 green
               dice 100 times. It would be expected that the number 6 would
               appear 1,000/ 6 = 167 times, whereas it actually showed up 188
               times. The difference between the expected and observed values is
               statistically significant (P = 0.04). Had this been the only
               result he reported, we could be excused if we insist that the
               casino give us only green dice to roll at the roulette table the
               next time we visit. However, it was just 1 part of a larger
               study, which involved doing the same procedure with 20 sets of
               dice, each of a different color. As it turned out, green was the
               only color for which there were more 6s than expected. What makes
               this P-hacking rather than a legitimate finding is that he did
               not begin his experiment by asking if the results for green dice
               would be different from those of other colors. Rather, he just
               amassed a lot of data and, without any hypotheses, searched for
               statistical significance; not surprisingly, with 20 statistical
               tests, he found it. Finding spurious effects has been shown in a
               number of contexts (some of them humorous), ranging from the
               association of having been born under a specific astrological
               sign with various medical conditions or responses to therapy, to
               fMRI patterns to visual stimuli recorded from a dead salmon.
               There is even a Web site devoted to graphs showing high
               correlations between totally unrelated variables, such as the per
               capita consumption of cheese and the number of peoplewho died by
               becoming tangled in their bedsheets (r = 0.947), the divorce rate
               inMaine and the per capita consumption of margarine (r = 0.993),
               and the total revenue generated by arcades and the number of
               computer science doctorates awarded in the U.S. (r = 0.985),
               proving not only that if you look hard enough, you will always
               find something correlated with something else, but also why you
               can never infer causality from a correlation. A related nefarious
               way to P-hack is to change the outcome from one that is looking
               unpromising to another one that looksmore likely toyield
               significance. Sad to say, this appears to be a pervasive problem",
  month     =  aug,
  year      =  2018,
  url       = "http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/JCP.0000000000000901",
  doi       = "10.1097/JCP.0000000000000901",
  pmid      =  29851710,
  issn      = "0271-0749,1533-712X",
  language  = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Streiner2018statistics-0006,
  title     = "Statistics commentary series: Commentary no. 29: Network
               meta-analysis",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J. Clin. Psychopharmacol.",
  publisher = "journals.lww.com",
  volume    =  38,
  number    =  6,
  pages     = "549--551",
  abstract  = "I n 2 of the previous commentaries, we discussed what
               meta-analysis (MA) is 1 and some of the decisions that must be
               made in conducting one. One might think that those articles would
               have exhausted the topic (if not the reader), but such is not the
               case. In 2002, Lumley introduced an extension of MA called
               “network meta-analysis” (NMA), which is also called “mixed
               treatments comparison” or “multiple treatments comparison”MA. The
               need for NMA is based on the fact that, for some disorders, there
               are fortunately many different therapies, and it is becoming
               increasingly infeasible to test each one against all of the
               others in head-to-head comparisons. NMA allows us to compare
               treatment Awith treatment B, even if they have not been compared
               directly in a randomized controlled trial (RCT), as long as both
               of them have been tested against a common comparator, such as a
               placebo or another treatment. The reason for the alias “mixed
               treatments comparison” is that the statistics of NMA allow us to
               combine the results of any studies that did in fact compare Awith
               B with the results of each against the common comparator, as seen
               in Figure 1. In the simplest case, we would have trials of A and
               B each versus C (either a third treatment or a placebo). If A is
               shown to be better than C, and C is better than B, then it may
               seem safe to conclude that A is better than B (ie, A > C > B).
               Even if there’s no difference between B and C, it would appear
               likely that A is better than B (A > C = B). However, this
               approach treats A and B as if they were individual arms from the
               same RCT, rather than from different studies. By ignoring the
               errors inherent in each study, it increases the liability to bias
               and may result in overly precise estimates of the true
               difference. The use of NMAwould eliminate this problem. NMA is
               particularly useful when there are other treatments, such as D,
               E, or F, forming a network of estimated treatment effects (hence
               the N in NMA). A (relatively) straightforward network, involving
               4 treatments, is illustrated in the top part of Figure 2, whereD
               can be another study drug or placebo. A is better than B and C;
               and both B and C are better than D. Also, comparing the
               differences between A and B (ΔAB) and ΔAC, and between ΔBD and
               ΔCD, we may be able to draw some conclusions about the difference
               between B and C. Here, too, NMA would be unnecessary, and a
               simple MAwould suffice. NMA becomes necessary, however, when we
               get inconsistent results among studies (which Lumley call
               “incoherence”), as seen in the bottom half of Figure 2. Combining
               ΔAB with ΔBD shows that A is better thanD. But, combiningΔAC
               andΔCD says just the opposite, thatD is superior to A. The
               International Conference on Harmonization of the European
               Medicines Agency outlined a number of possible reasons for this
               incoherence. The first is that the trials of the various drugs
               may have recruited different participants. This is likely one
               reason for the inconsistency of results among trials of
               antidepressants, which may be effective for those with severe
               depression, but no better than placebo for patients with mild or
               moderate depression. A second reason could arise from
               shortcomings in the study design. For example, while most studies
               use double blinding with a placebo control group, it is often
               easy for patients to determine if they are taking an active drug
               or not, based on the presence or absence of side-effects such as
               dry mouth. Thus, part of the inconsistency may be due to the fact
               that blinding may have been compromised in some of the trials.
               There are many other ways in which studies may differ, including
               the duration of the trial, the choice of end-points
               (clinician-completed versus a self-rating scale), and so forth.
               What this means is that there are a number of sources of error
               that must be considered when estimating effects across many
               different treatments. The first is that, for each individual
               comparison, such as Aversus B, there will be sampling error from
               one study to the next. Thus, even if there are a number of trials
               that are perfect replicates of one another with regard to their
               sampling frame, sample size, choice of outcome measure, and so
               on, there will be some degree of heterogeneity across studies.
               Further, the efficacy of treatment A should be the same,
               irrespective of the treatment it is being compared with. The
               degree to which it varies from one comparator to the next is an
               index of the incoherence of the network. This is comparable to
               the heterogeneity of effect sizes (ESs) in",
  month     =  dec,
