@ARTICLE{Streiner2019statistics-0006,
  title     = "Statistics Commentary Series: Commentary No. 34: The Jackknife,
               Bootstrapping, and Monte Carlo Methods",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J Clin Psychopharmacol",
  publisher = "journals.lww.com",
  volume    =  39,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "426--427",
  abstract  = "S tatisticians are ecstatic when the data that they’re analyzing
               can be assumed to be normally distributed. They are also happy
               (albeit possibly a tad less so) when the data follow other known
               distributions, such as the Poisson, negative binomial,
               hypergeometric, or some other one. The reason is that the
               formulae for these distributions are known and so we can model
               what the data should look like and thus use tests of
               significance. But nature isn’t always kind to us, and there are
               times when the data cannot easily be described by some formula,
               or when the assumptions of parametric tests (ie, those where we
               know the parameters of the distribution) aren’t fully met. What
               are statisticians to do in these circumstances? Not wanting to be
               foiled by such piddling constraints as assumptions, they have
               devised a series of techniques that do not require that we know
               the distribution, or that the distribution meets all of the
               assumptions. As with most things in statistics, the path was
               first blazed by Sir Ronald Fisher. Although the chisquared (χ)
               test is often described as non-parametric, this isn’t quite
               accurate. Because the data in the cells are counts, they are
               assumed to follow a Poisson distribution, which they tend to do
               as long as the expected frequency in each cell is at least 5. If
               there aren’t at least 5, then we use Fisher’s exact test, which
               is a permutation test. This works out every possible layout of
               the data in a 2x2 table (ie, starting with the actual number of
               observations in the smallest cell, then decreasing it by 1, and
               then 2, and so on down to 0), and calculates the probability of
               each; the final value is the sum of these probabilities. In fact,
               we can calculate the exact test for larger tables and when the
               expected frequency is greater than 5, but the calculations become
               mind-boggling, especially in the days prior to computers and
               calculators with builtin factorial functions, which is why Fisher
               abandoned this approach. This approach is called a “resampling”
               method, because the same data are resampled for each calculation.
               The next step in the evolution of resampling statistics, the
               jackknife, was developed by Quenouille, who developed it to
               determine howmuchmultiple regression and other procedures were
               affected by outliers. It was given its sobriquet by John Tukey,
               because he saw it as an all-purpose tool that can be used for
               many things, much like a real jackknife. How it works is
               explained by its other name, leave-one-out. The data are analyzed
               multiple times, each time omitting 1 data point. Thus, the first
               time, the data from subjects 2 through n are analyzed, omitting
               subject 1; then subjects 1 and 3 through n are used, leaving out
               subject 2; and so forth. We can then determine not only the means
               of the estimates of, say, the coefficients of a multiple
               regression, but also how much the estimates vary due to outlying
               values. The major drawback of this approach is that given n
               subjects, we’re restricted to n samples from the data. This may
               be sufficient when the sample size is very large, but can be a
               problem if there are only 50 or so subjects, which can limit the
               accuracy of our estimates. Efron and Tibshirani avoided this
               limitation with a technique called bootstrapping, because we use
               the data to lift ourselves up by our bootstraps (not something
               you should try in real life).With this technique, we draw
               repeated samples of the same size from the datawith replacement.
               That is, if say subject 25 is included in the sample, her data
               point remains in the dataset, so it can be used again (and
               possibly yet again) in the same sample. That means that we can
               draw an unlimited number of samples from the dataset, and each
               wouldmost likely include different subgroups of people. Indeed,
               it’s not unusual to draw 1,000 or even 10,000 samples from a
               dataset with far fewer subjects. For example, let’s assume we do
               a small pilot project to determine the proportion of patients who
               improve after treatment with a certain drug, and we find that 12
               out of 18 do so. Is it worth proceeding with a large randomized
               controlled study? A success rate of 66.7\% looks fairly
               impressive, but with only 18 subjects, how confident arewe that
               this is an accurate estimate of the drug’s effect? Using
               bootstrapping, we can draw 1,000 samples of 18 people each.
               Because this is done with replacement, each of the people will be
               used many times, but the composition of each sample will change
               from one draw to the next. For example, in 1 sample, subject 7
               may appear 130 times, but subject 11 may be included only 50
               times. The next samplewould consist of a different combination of
               some people appearing verymany times and others being included
               less often. If we did this 4 times, we may find that the answers
               are 67.2\%, 76.7\%, 60.0\%,",
