               this monograph was too vague to lead to a different approach to
               statistics. However, it caught the eye of a brilliant
               mathematician, Pierre-Simon Marquis de Laplace, who turned it
               into something that statisticians could work with (and fight
               over). Bayesian statistics languished for many years, derided by
               traditional statisticians, but has recently been resurrected as a
               powerful tool in diagnosis and data analysis. So what are
               Bayesian statistics, and why have they generated so much
               controversy over the years? To understand this, it’s necessary to
               compare them to the more widely used techniques, which are called
               frequentist statistics. To do so, let’s imagine a study. A line
               is drawn down a wall, but you don’t know where it is –whether
               it’s down the center, off to the right, or toward the left. You
               are blindfolded and given 100 darts to throw at the wall (you can
               use darts with suction cups at the end if you’re worried about
               damaging the wall). Your initial belief may be that the line is
               in the center, which is probably the safest assumption in the
               absence of any other information. If you are a frequentist, you
               would throw all of the darts and state your belief only at the
               end of the study. If significantly more darts landed to the left
               of the line, you would reject the null hypothesis (that is, the
               hypothesis to be nullified) and state that the line was closer to
               the right wall, leaving more room for the darts to land on the
               left. To the frequentist, the hypothesis is fixed, and the data
               are used to either support or reject it. A Bayesian would also
               start with the same prior belief, but would be told after each
               throw where the dart landed. If the first throw is to the left of
