  pages     = "109--111",
  abstract  = "I n a previous commentary, 1 I mentioned Simpson's paradox; a
               situation which arises when there is a trend in some studies that
               is reversed or eliminated when the results of the different
               studies are combined. For example, imagine that there were 2
               studies that looked at the relationship between the use of
               psychotropic medication and falls in the elderly; the results of
               the studies are shown in Table 1. There is a very strong
               association between falls and medication use in study A, withχ(1)
               = 11.88, P < 0.001, as well as in study B [χ(1) = 7.13, P <
               0.008]. If we calculate the relative risk (RR) for each study, we
               would find it is 1.50 in the first study and 1.19 in the second.
               Something strange happens, though, when we pool the results, as
               shown in Table 2. Now, it looks as if there is no relationship at
               all [χ(1) = 1.54, P = 0.214], and in fact, there is a slight
               trend for medication use to lead to fewer falls (56\% vs 61\%),
               with a RR of 0.93. This is not just a theoretical problem. In
               1973, the University of California, Berkeley, was sued for
               alleged bias against women. As evidence, the plaintiffs stated
               that 44\% of males who applied to the university were admitted,
               whereas the acceptance rate for females was only 35\%. In its
               defense, the university presented the data in Table 3. In 3 of
               the departments (A, B, and D), in fact a higher proportion of
               women were admitted; 2 departments (C and E) admitted a higher
               proportion of men, and there was no difference in department F.
               Sowhat is going on? It turns out that women tend to apply to
               departments that have more applicants and therefore lower
               acceptance rates (eg, English, social sciences), whereas men
               applied to programs that had less competition (eg, physics,
               engineering). Pooling the data, which ignored these differences
               between departments, resulted in totals that were not reflective
               of the actual situation. In the case of the studies of medication
               and falls in the first example, pooling the data ignored the
               differences between them—study A had nearly 21⁄2 times the number
               of people taking medications than not taking them, whereas in
               study B, the situation was reversed, in that there were nearly 4
               times as many people not using psychotropic drugs as using them.
               This likely reflects a “lurking variable,” or confounder; that
               is, some factor that makes the studies different, such as
               differing definitions of falls, differences in demographic
               characteristics of the people, and so forth. The lurking variable
               in the Berkeley example was department, which had different
               acceptance rates for males and females. Let us take a look at
               another situation that can result in Simpson's paradox. We will
               assume (safely) that there is an inverse relationship between
               wealth and depression—those with fewer financial resources tend
               to be more depressed—and that this relationship holds for both
               men and women. Two other assumptions are that women report more
               depressive symptoms than men (which is true) and that women have
               more wealth than men (debatable, but let us assume it for now).
               This is seen in the 2 scatterplots in Figure 1: there is a
               negative correlation between wealth and depression for both men
               and women (r = −0.91 for each), reflected in the solid regression
               lines running through each of the scatterplots. But now, let us
               correlate the 2 variables, combining males and females. What we
               find, shown by the dashed regression line, is a strong positive
               correlation between wealth and depression (r = 0.68), meaning
               that the more wealth you have, the sadder you are. This is a
               prime example of Simpson's paradox; the negative correlations,
               found when the groups are analyzed separately, become a positive
               onewhen the groups are combined. It also shows the reason for the
               paradox, which is the same as in the first 2 examples—it arises
               when a lurking or confounding variable (sex, in this case) is
               ignored. So, how can we protect ourselves from Simpson's paradox?
               The primary way is the most difficult, because it requires
               thinking and knowledge rather than statistics. It is to be aware
               of any possible confounders. A confounder is any variable,
               lurking in the background, that is (a) correlated with the
               independent variable (IV), (b) correlated with the dependent
               variable (DV), and (c) not part of the causal pathway between the
               IV and DV. When such a variable is suspected to exist, the design
               of the study or its analysis must take this into account.",
