@ARTICLE{Streiner2020statistics,
  title     = "Statistics commentary series. Commentary no. 41: Randomization:
               Randomization",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J. Clin. Psychopharmacol.",
  publisher = "Ovid Technologies (Wolters Kluwer Health)",
  volume    =  40,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "439--440",
  abstract  = "T he hallmark of the randomized controlled trial is, not
               surprisingly, the random assignment of subjects to groups.
               Randomization is done for 3 reasons. First, most statistical
               tests, such as the analysis of covariance, are predicated on the
               assumption that participants have been randomized to the various
               groups, although in reality this is more honored in the breach
               than in the observance. Second, while it does not guarantee that
               the groups will be perfectly balanced in terms of both known and
               unknown confounders, it maximizes the chances that they will be.
               Finally, it prevents people from “gaming the system.” That is, if
               it is known that a particular study allocates subjects who have
               been referred on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday to the new
               treatment, and those referred on Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday
               to receive either the usual treatment or placebo, then clinicians
               who have faith in the new treatment will refer their patients
               only on days when they will be assigned to that group; while
               clinicians who are skeptical of it will refer on the other days,
               most likely subverting the balance of covariates that
               randomization is designed to achieve. When we think of
               randomization (if we think of it at all), we imagine flipping a
               coin for each participant and assigning him or her to 1 group if
               it comes up heads and to the other group if tails. When we have
               more than 2 groups, we can use either a table of random numbers
               or a computer-generated list to make the assignments. If there
               are 3 groups, for example, the numbers 1, 2, and 3 would indicate
               group A; 4, 5, and 6 would be group B; and 7, 8, and 9 would be
               the third group (0 would just be ignored). However, this simple
