@ARTICLE{Streiner2020statistics-0003,
  title     = "Statistics commentary series. Commentary no. 42: Minimization:
               Minimization",
  author    = "Streiner, David L",
  journal   = "J. Clin. Psychopharmacol.",
  publisher = "Ovid Technologies (Wolters Kluwer Health)",
  volume    =  40,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "441--442",
  abstract  = "I n previous commentaries, we have extolled the virtues of
               randomization as the ideal method of allocating subjects to
               groups. The reasons are 3-fold: many statistical tests require
               it; it prevents allocation bias (that is, clinicians subverting
               the randomization process by referring patients to their
               preferred group); and it minimizes the chances of confounders
               being unequally distributed among the groups. You might surmise
               from this that randomization and its variants – block
               randomization and stratified randomization – are the only game in
               town, but this would be wrong. In fact, there’s another method
               that achieves the same aim and in a more efficient manner, albeit
               1 that is not very widely used:minimization. In this article, we
               will discuss what minimization is, some reasons it is not used
               more commonly, and some reasons that it should be. Minimization
               is a form of dynamic, covariate adaptive randomization. It is
               dynamic because, rather than having a randomization scheme
               established before the trial begins, it changes as each person is
               randomized; and it is covariate adaptive because it takes into
               account the allocations of the previous participants in order to
               best balance the covariates. (In this latter regard, it differs
               from response adaptive trials, described in a previous
               commentary, in which allocation depends on the results of interim
               analyses.) Whereas stratification uses mutually exclusive
               subgroups, minimization seeks to minimize the differences between
               the groups by considering all of the factors simultaneously. Its
               main advantage over stratified randomization is its ability to
               usemore than 2 or 3 covariates, which is about the maximum that
               is feasiblewith stratification, especially if the pool of
               possible participants is limited. In fact, Taves first introduced
               minimization (and gave it its name) with an example using 15
               covariates, some of which had as many as 8 strata.
               Coincidentally, Pocock and Simon proposed a very similar scheme
               at almost the same time. To see how it works, let’s use only 3
               variables that we are interested in balancing between 2 groups –
               sex, previous hospitalizations (yes/no), and currently on
               medication (yes/no). As mentioned, minimization can handle many
               more, but 3 will suffice for illustrative purposes. To start the
               process using Taves’ method, the first 2 to 10 participants are
               assigned to the various groups purely at random. Let’s assume
               that after 10 patients have been randomized, the distribution of
               the covariates is as described in Table 1. Patient 11, who is now
               ready to be assigned, is a female, who has been previously
               hospitalized, and is currently on medications. The subtotal for
               this combination of covariates in Treatment 1 is 4 (for female) +
               3 (for hospitalized) + 1 (on medications) = 8. In Treatment 2,
               the subtotal is 1 + 2 + 4 = 7 for these covariates. Because the
               subtotal is lower in group 2, assigning her to this group would
               better balance the covariates. If the subtotals were equal, then
               the assignment would be made randomly. Pocock and Simon’s method
               is somewhat more complicated, but generally produces very similar
               results. Its main advantage is that the various prognostic
               factors can be weighted if some are deemed more important than
               others. As mentioned, the main advantages of randomization is
               that it provides better balance between the groups compared with
               simple randomization and its ability to incorporate many more
               covariates than can stratification. These factors are
               particularly important in smaller trials, where the pool of
               eligible participants is limited, and unrestricted randomization
               may result in imbalances among the covariates. When there are
               fewer than 400 patients in the study, minimization always
               resulted in better balanced groups than simple randomization, but
               there was little difference in simulations when the sample size
               approached 1,000. One problem with minimization is that once the
               participant’s covariates are known, it can be determined to which
               group he or she should be assigned, thus allowing for the
               possibility of gaming the system and undermining the assignment.
               (This is also a problem with permuted block randomization, when
               the allocation of the final person in each block can be perfectly
               predicted.) For this reason, a “fudge factor” is often added.
               This is a probability, often around 0.70 to 0.80, that is
               multiplied by the group assignment, so that the group assignment
               cannot be perfectly predicted. In this case, for example, there
               would be only a 70\% to 80\% chance that the patient would be
               placed in group 2. A second problem is that minimization is not
               purely random allocation method, which is an underlying
               assumption of most statistical tests. It has been recommended
               bymany that it is therefore necessary",
