               Piltdown man alive and well?”), outright lying (“Lies, damned
               lies, and medical science”), or calling into question the entire
               scientific enterprise (“Many scientific “truths” are, in fact,
               false”). Partly on the basis of the Ioannidis article, the
               Reproducibility Project: Psychology, launched in 2011, attempted
               to replicate 100 published studies in the field (only 98 were,
               because 2 teams duplicated other studies). With some degree of
               horror, Nature News reported that the results for over half the
               studies could not be confirmed. Although the inability to
               replicate results is indeed a problem in medicine and other
               fields, we believe that this does not inmost cases
               indicatewrongdoing or even poor research practices. Rather, it
               reflects a misunderstanding of the meaning of P ≤ 0.05 and, in
               particular, the probability of achieving a statistically
               significant outcome when replicating a study. The issue can be
               phrased in 1 of 2 ways: (1) given that a study has found a
               significant result at some P level, what is the probability that
               a replication will be significant; and (2) if a study reports a
               confidence interval (CI) of a particular width (usually 95\%),
               what is the probability that the means of replications will fall
               within that interval? The answer to the first question depends in
               part on the power of the second study; that is, its sample size.
               But to keep things simple (at least at first), let’s make a few
               assumptions: the second study is an exact duplicate of the first,
               meaning that the power of the 2 studies is the same; and the
               results are expressed as z scores, so that the expected mean
               under the null hypothesis is 0 and the distribution of all
               replication means will be normal with a variance of 1. To begin
               with, let’s further assume that the first study was significant
               at exactly P = 0.05. This situation is depicted in Figure 1. The
               normal curve on the left, drawn with dashed lines, shows the
               distribution of scores we would expect if the null hypothesis
               were true. The solid normal curve on the right is the
               distribution of scores wewould expect if the difference between
               the means were significant at exactly the 0.05 level. The shaded
               portion of that curve shows that half of the replications would
