  abstract = "Longitudinal cohort studies can provide important evidence about
              preventable causes of disease, but the success relies heavily on
              the commitment of their participants, both at recruitment and
              during follow up. Initial participation rates have decreased in
              recent decades as have willingness to participate in subsequent
              follow ups. It is important to examine how such selection affects
