               limitations of the range of methods now available, and makes good
               practice recommendations for researchers, funders, publishers,
               and users of evidence. Unlike true experiments that are conducted
               by researchers for scientific purposes, natural experiments occur
               when infrastructure, policies, or services are introduced or
               changed by governments or healthcare systems. Interventions of
               this kind are sometimes amenable to randomised controlled trials,
               for example, if the advantages of randomisation can be negotiated
               with policy makers or providers at the planning stage and the
               findings are likely to be transferable across several contexts.
               Although the randomised controlled trial remains an important
