               Medicine that describes attrition bias and outlines its potential
               impact on research studies and the preventive steps to minimise
               its risk. Attrition bias is a type of selection bias due to
               systematic differences between study groups in the number and the
               way participants are lost from a study. Differences between
               people who leave a study and those who continue, particularly
               between study groups, can be the reason for any observed effect
               and not the intervention itself. Associations for mortality in
               trials of tranexamic acid and upper gastrointestinal bleeding
               were no longer apparent after studies with high or unclear risk
               of attrition bias were removed. Over-recruitment can help prevent
