  volume   =  5,
  number   =  1,
  pages    =  54,
  abstract = "BACKGROUND: Composite responder endpoints feature frequently in
              rheumatology due to the multifaceted nature of many of these
              conditions. Current analysis methods used to analyse these
              endpoints discard much of the data used to classify patients as
              responders and are therefore highly inefficient, resulting in low
              power. We highlight a novel augmented methodology that uses more
              of the information available to improve the precision of reported
              treatment effects. Since these methods are more challenging to
              implement, we developed free, user-friendly software available in
              a web-based interface and as R packages. The software consists of
              two programs: one that supports the analysis of responder
              endpoints; the second that facilitates sample size estimation. We
              demonstrate the use of the software to conduct the analysis with
              both the augmented and standard analysis method using the MUSE
              study, a phase IIb trial in patients with systemic lupus
              erythematosus. RESULTS: The software outputs similar point
              estimates with smaller confidence intervals for the odds ratio,
              risk ratio and risk difference estimators using the augmented
              approach. The sample size required in each arm for a future trial
              using the novel approach based on the MUSE data is 50 versus 135
              for the standard method, translating to a reduction in required
              sample size of approximately 63\%. CONCLUSIONS: We encourage
              trialists to use the software demonstrated to implement the
              augmented methodology in future studies to improve efficiency.",
  month    =  dec,
  year     =  2021,
